## Use with vLLM (recommended)

```python
from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams


def main():
    llm = LLM(
        model="{{ repo_id }}",
        quantization="glq",
        dtype="bfloat16",
        max_model_len=4096,
{% if multimodal %}
        limit_mm_per_prompt={"image": 0, "video": 0, "audio": 0},  # text-only serving
{% endif %}
    )
    out = llm.generate(["The capital city of New Zealand is"],
                       SamplingParams(max_tokens=64, temperature=0))
    print(out[0].outputs[0].text)


# The __main__ guard is REQUIRED when running this as a script. vLLM switches to the
# "spawn" multiprocessing start method once CUDA is initialised, so its worker processes
# re-import this file; without the guard the script spawns itself recursively and dies
# with "An attempt has been made to start a new process before the current process has
# finished its bootstrapping phase" — before the model ever loads.
if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

`glq` registers with vLLM automatically via its plugin entry point — no extra import needed.

{% if is_moe %}
## Fit it on a smaller card — weight offload (vLLM ≥ 0.30.0)

vLLM can keep weights in pinned host RAM and let the GPU read them over PCIe. This model is a
**mixture of experts**, which is the case where that is cheap: only the routed experts are read
per token, so experts sitting in host memory cost nothing until they are selected. Restrict the
offload to them with `--cpu-offload-params experts`.

```bash
vllm serve {{ repo_id }} \
    --quantization glq \
    --offload-backend uva \
    --cpu-offload-gb 24 \
    --cpu-offload-params experts \
    --max-model-len {{ max_ctx }}{% if has_ple %} \
    --max-num-seqs 1{% endif %}{% if multimodal %} \
    --limit-mm-per-prompt '{"image": 0, "video": 0, "audio": 0}'{% endif %}

```

`--cpu-offload-gb` is the host-memory budget in GiB — raise it to free more VRAM, at the cost
of decode speed. `--cpu-offload-params` takes **one** value; `experts,mlp` is read as a single
literal string, not a list. Use `uva`, not the `prefetch` backend: prefetch bulk-copies whole
layer groups, so it transfers experts that were never routed and loses the saving.

`--max-model-len` is the longest sequence the engine will accept — prompt plus generated
tokens — and it sizes the KV reservation per sequence. {{ max_ctx }} is this checkpoint's
declared ceiling. Offload makes a long context **more** affordable rather than less, because
the VRAM the weights give up goes to the KV pool.

**`--max-model-len` and `--max-num-seqs` trade against each other**, and at full context the
trade is steep: concurrency is roughly `KV pool ÷ max-model-len`. Pick one and derive the
other from what vLLM reports as `GPU KV cache size` at startup — it prints both that and a
`Maximum concurrency for N tokens per request` line. `--max-num-seqs 1` above is the
conservative pairing for full context; if you serve shorter requests, lower `--max-model-len`
and raise `--max-num-seqs` together, because sequences only consume the KV they actually use.
{% if has_ple %}

This checkpoint also has a per-layer-embedding (PLE) table, which GLQ can hold in host memory
independently. The two stack — set `VLLM_PLE_CPU_OFFLOAD=1` (it is vLLM's default) and the PLE
table does **not** count against `--cpu-offload-gb`. Note that each in-flight sequence reserves
recurrent-layer state on the GPU, so leave headroom for it: a budget that loads the weights can
still be too small to serve a request.
{% endif %}
